   "source": [
    "The ANOVA returns **F ≈ 54.6 with p ≈ 2 × 10⁻⁵⁶**, decisively rejecting the null hypothesis at any conventional α level. The per-ticker summary makes the source of the difference obvious: NVDA's average 20-day forward return is **+5.42%**, more than double every other ticker — AAPL +2.29%, AMZN +2.08%, MSFT +2.08%, GOOGL +2.04%, META +1.96%. The boxplot also shows visibly fatter tails for NVDA and META, indicating these names contribute disproportionately to the cross-sectional variance. The five non-NVDA tickers cluster tightly around +2%; NVDA is the dominant outlier.\n",
    "\n",
    "This is the strongest single-test justification for the team's ML design. Because mean forward returns differ so sharply across tickers, the model needs ticker identity as a feature — which is exactly why the team **one-hot encodes the ticker** before training (`Ticker_AAPL`, `Ticker_AMZN`, …, `Ticker_NVDA` become independent columns the Random Forest can split on). The cross-ticker variance heterogeneity also motivates two more engineered feature families: the **z-scored Volume and Return features** make magnitudes comparable across tickers of different scale, and the **Market_Return / Relative_Strength features** subtract the cross-sectional FAANG+ mean so the model can isolate ticker-idiosyncratic signal from the broad-market drift this ANOVA detects. Combined with Method 1's finding that no single indicator carries strong linear signal, the picture is clear: the predictive structure in this dataset is *ticker-conditional*, and the ML pipeline is built to exploit that."
   ]
  },
