   "source": [
    "Similarily to data we obtained from Exploratory Data Analysis, Random Forest continues to be the best option. The data above reinforces this statement as\n",
    "from the output, we can see that KNN and Logistic Regression perform the worst, having 0.672 and 0.608 accuracy. Although accuracy isn't the main focus for an imbalanced dataset, both KNN and Logistic Regression have lower precision, recall, and f1-scores relative to Decision Tree and Random Forest. Finally, all precision, recall, and f1-score values for Random Forest are greater than Decision Tree. Even if we were to solely focus on the positive increases, Random Forest still out competes Decision Tree. As a result, Random Forest is again the model we want to consider moving forward with. The models above have no parameters optimized to prevent overfitting and underfitting, as we are solely comparing the baseline to determine the model we want to utilize. Therefore, for Random Forest, we need to truncate and add restriction to prevent overfitting using new parameters.\n",
    "\n",
    "Optimized Hyperparameters:<br>\n",
    "**n_estimators** is the number of descision trees our random forest will create. For this value we will choose 300.<br>\n",
    "**max_depth** is the max number of layers each tree can have. This prevents overfiting. For this values we will choose 10.<br>\n",
    "**class_weight** is used for imbalanced data sets. Since we are only considering improvements of 1% or more for the positive case, our data will be slightly inbalanced.<br>\n",
    "**min_samples_split** is the number of samples needed before a node can split. Adding this prevents a tree from splitting into abnormal small groups. We will choose 10.<br>\n",
    "**min_samples_leaf** is the number of samples each leaf needs to have. Adding this restriction can reduce overfitting and we will choose 5.<br>\n",
    "**random_state** is a seed for random. We will use 42.<br>\n",
    "\n",
    "These parameters are all optimized in consideration of overfitting. Since our dataset is large, larger parameter values are to be expected. Still, values like max_depth if set to greater than around like 15 would definitely overfit. Therefore, we found balance between performance and overfitting."
   ]
