  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Model Analysis on the Test Set:<br>\n",
    "The final test results suggest that Random Tree was able to obtain an accuracy of 0.7225, which isn't too bad for predictions on stock data. Since accuracy isn't the most important datapoint in an imbalanced dataset, we need to look toward precision and recall. Ideally, we would want precision to be higher, prioritizing the prevention of false positives. We would much rather have the model not catch a rising stock than state a stock that rises will actually fall. For example, a consumer would much rather have an unlucky missed opportunity than to lose a significant amount of money believing in the prediction model. So to check if we succeeded in some manner, lets look into both the classification report and the confusion matrix.\n",
    "\n",
    "The classification report suggests that a prediction of the stock price decreasing within the next month has 0.65 precision and 0.67 recall. For the purpose of the model, a 0.65 precision is alright, meaning that more times than not the algorithm identifies a correct decrease. A 0.67 recall is also alright, meaning the model is actually catching stocks that will decrease more times than not.\n",
    "\n",
    "Now lets look towards the positive case, which is an increase in the stock price next month. The precision value is 0.78 and the recall value is 0.75 with an f1-score of 0.77. As a result, for the test data, many of the rising stocks within the next month were actually detected while those that were detected had a high chance of actually going up. As a result, the model is working much more effectively for detecting an increase in the next month. Having an f1-score of 0.77 also suggests there is an above average balance between precision and recall. Therefore, the model does well with predicting the positive case.\n",
    "\n",
    "The confusion matrix will be used to corroborate the findings from the classification report. We can see that the bottom right, true positives (1326), are significantly larger than the other data points. Therefore, this reinforces the high recall score, 0.75, for the positive case. The value in the top left is lower than the bottom right (793), corresponding to the lower recall, 0.67, of the negative case. The top right suggest the number of stocks that actually decreased but were predicted to increase. The value isn't minimal but pretty low (383) which makes sense, corresponding to the higher precision value of 0.78. Finally, the number of false negatives (431), stocks determined to decrease but increase, is slightly higher than the false positives, corresponding accurately to the the precision for the 0 case being 0.65.\n",
    "\n",
    "Therefore, from both the classification report and the confusion matrix, we can say the model worked pretty well. Even though the accuracy, precision, and recall values aren't exactly ideal (0.9 or more), stock data can be random and have a lot of noise. To prevent overfitting, the accuracy scores of both the trained data and test data are obtained. For stock data, it is normal for there to be more noise and datapoints. Therefore, we aimed to keep the difference of the accuracy of the two sets near 0.1 (0.8257608047054812 - 0.7224684623252642 = 0.10329234238).\n",
    "\n",
    "In conclusion, the question (will the price of the stock of these companies (Meta, Apple, Amazon, Nvidia, Microsoft, Google) increase or decrease one month from now) can be answered by our model with correct predictions for 72.25% on test data, meaning there is a possibility for machine learning to be optimized to predict stock price rises or falls in the next month."
   ]
  },
