   "source": [
    "After reading through our implementation of a ML model that determines the next months stock price on FAANG+ data, we hope that the tutorial has taught readers two things. First, machine learning models can be utilized to make predictions on stock prices (monthly) and can continue to improve similarily to how our model improved through feature analysis. Second, although a model can be made, a successful model is still rather difficult to produce. Our model only reached 73.4% accuracy on unseen data, but the data was still from the same dataset. As a result, for random new stock data, there will be new challenges, further reducing the accuracy from the decent 73.4% accuracy we obtained. The lesson learned is that although machine learning can achieve new heights in predicting stock trends, algorithms are not full proof and require constant updating and risks.\n",
    "\n",
    "Furthermore, through building the model, we recognize there are difficult constraints on machine learning and that machines have computational power that can further limit its prowess. For example, when testing hyperparameters for Random Forest, there was difficulty balancing overfitting, underfitting, and accuracy results. When raising the max depth of the Random Forest, the data kept performing extremely well on trained data, but was only slightly performing better on test data. As a result, knowing the noisiness of stocks, the group chose to keep accuracies closer to represent realistic functionality on a stock market that wasn't always ideal. As for computational power, there is a huge drawback in runtime. As we improved our model, even though the model isn't exactly expensive, the runtime jumped significantly higher.\n",
    "\n",
    "In conclusion, we are proud of the resulting accuracy of our model, answering that it is possible in some sense to predict next months stock movement. We were able to reach values near 0.7 or higher for precision, recall, and accuracy, which is pretty high considering the large amount of noise from large stock data. Hopefully, readers can use the model's predictions and analysis to produce models that can be far more efficient than ours, expanding the demonstration of this project into a real-world basis."
